US-009 done. One thing worth flagging up front:

**The production bug was already fixed by US-008, in the opposite direction to what this story specified.** The story asked `/sell` to migrate to POST "like the history already did"; US-008 did the inverse — it made the route export only `GET` (idempotent read, no body) and converted the history's two `<form method="POST">` into links. That incidentally fixed `app/sell/page.tsx`, whose `<a href>` had been hitting a 405, without touching it. I treated the criterion *"coherent with the interface resulting from US-008"* as governing over the literal *"responds to POST and not GET"*, so the test pins the contract in the real direction: **GET yes, POST no**. No changes were needed in `app/sell/page.tsx` or `app/history/page.tsx` — both already use the same mechanism.

What I added:

- **`app/api/invoices/[saleId]/route.test.ts`** — asserts the module's verb contract: `typeof route.GET === "function"` and `expect(route).not.toHaveProperty("POST")`. Next answers 405 for any verb without an exported handler, so this is exactly the assertion that would have gone red under the original bug. It has to be tested against the *module*, not a response — the 405 comes from Next's router, not the file.
- **`app/sell/page.test.tsx`** — new `describe` that runs the sale through to "¡Venta confirmada!" and checks the control is a `role="link"` with `href === "/api/invoices/sale-1"`. This is the Vitest coverage for "uses the same mechanism as the history": turning it into a `<button>`/`<form>` breaks `getByRole("link")`.
- **`e2e/comercio-flow.spec.ts`** — the step now **clicks** the link instead of asserting visibility, and awaits the `/api/invoices/` response, asserting `200` and `content-type: application/pdf`. A 405 fails it. Since the link is `target="_blank"`, the request comes from the new tab, so it listens on `page.context()`, not `page` (`BrowserContext` doesn't emit `download`, only `Page` does).

**Validation:** `npm run typecheck`, `npm run lint`, and `npm test` (165 tests, 13 files) all pass. `tsconfig.json` includes `**/*.ts`, so typecheck did cover the new Playwright call signature. I did **not** run `npm run test:e2e` — it creates real organizations in the inventory service and orphans them, and the story states it isn't a quality gate; the e2e change is verified only by typecheck. No i18n changes; no visual check since there were no UI changes.

